# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: namespace-storage-blocks-ci — the release lanes cannot clone, because keeping release artefacts for ever filled a free namespace
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Symptom

From 14:42 on 2026-08-21, every CI job dies in =get_sources=, on every
runner and every ref, in a handful of seconds:

#+begin_example
error: RPC failed; HTTP 403 curl 22 The requested URL returned error: 403
fatal: expected flush after ref listing
ERROR: Job failed: exit code 128
#+end_example

It took the five release lanes of the =release-1.9.2= tag with it, so
=collect:release= was skipped and the release could not be published. The
same minute, merge-request jobs died the same way — so it is not the tag,
not the release, and not a =.gitlab-ci.yml= change.

* Cause — the namespace is over its storage limit

: namespace  ogamita        plan: free
: project    clautolisp     job_artifacts_size  23.14 GB
:                           storage_size        23.18 GB

A free GitLab.com namespace includes *10 GB*. Over the limit, GitLab
refuses git operations — which is exactly a 403 on the fetch, with no
message about storage anywhere in the job log. That silence is why this
looked for a while like a CI-token or configuration fault.

*It is not today's doing alone.* Of those 23 GB, only *3.26 GB* is in the
last 1500 jobs; the rest is older history. Today's release attempts were
the last straw, not the cause.

* The design tension, which is the real subject

The single heaviest object in the project is

: 952 MB   collect:release   release-1.9.0

and it is *supposed* to be there for ever. =collect:release= carries
=expire_in: never= deliberately, because a published Release LINKS AT its
artefacts: with an expiry, every release turns into dead links some weeks
later (release-asset-links-use-artifacts-file.issue, and
=make check-release= enforces the =never=).

So the rule that keeps releases usable also means *every release costs
about a gigabyte, permanently*. On a 10 GB namespace that is a budget of
roughly ten releases before git itself stops working — and 1.9.0, 1.9.1
and 1.9.2 spent three of them in one day.

The two rules are individually right and jointly unsustainable. That is
the thing to fix, not the symptom.

* What can be freed, and what must not be

| artefact | may be deleted? |
|----------+-----------------|
| =documentation= (1.04 GB across ~20 jobs, 52 MB each, one per pipeline) | YES — no consumer after the job |
| =build:alfe:*=, =test:*=, probes | YES — 14-day expiry anyway |
| per-target =release:*= of superseded tags | YES — collect has already unioned them |
| =collect:release= of a PUBLISHED release | *NO* — the Release page links straight at it |

=collect:release= for =release-1.9.0= is the awkward one: it is 952 MB,
it belongs to a release whose Windows products do not work and which
1.9.2 supersedes, and deleting it would turn that release's 15 asset
links into 404s. Whether a superseded, broken release keeps its assets is
a judgement for pjb, not a cleanup script.

* Options, none chosen

1. *Purge old job artefacts* (everything but published =collect:release=).
   Frees most of the ~20 GB of history immediately and unblocks CI. Costs
   nothing that has a consumer; irreversible without re-running pipelines.
2. *Stop shipping the combined =-all.zip= / =-all.tar.bz2=.* They are 276 MB
   and 246 MB of the 952 MB, and they duplicate the per-target archives
   byte for byte. Halves the permanent cost of every future release.
3. *Publish release assets outside job artefacts* — the GitLab Package
   Registry, or a release-assets project. Removes the expiry problem at
   the root, and is the only option that scales past ten releases; also
   the most work.
4. *Raise the namespace allowance.* Money instead of engineering, and
   legitimate, but it defers rather than answers.

Option 1 is the emergency; option 2 is the cheapest real reduction;
option 3 is the answer if releases are to keep being cut at this rate.

* Note

Found while cutting release-1.9.2, by measuring rather than guessing: the
job log says only "403", the project statistics say 23.14 GB, and the
namespace says "free". Worth writing down because the next person to meet
this will see a 403 on a git fetch and go looking at tokens, which is
where the first hour went here.
